class:: FFTSubbandFlux2
summary:: Spectral flux in independently bounded bands
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/SpecCentroid2, Classes/SpecFlatness2

description::
Each band returns sqrt(sum((w - previousW).squared)). Positive mode clips each difference at zero first. Normalized mode divides by sqrt(sum(w.squared)), with a zero norm giving zero. The first FFT frame returns zero and establishes the history. In these equations w is magnitude or squared magnitude. Adjacent bands include their lower boundary and exclude their upper boundary, except for the final band, which includes its upper boundary. Bands outside Nyquist or without any bin centers return 0.

Peeters (2004), A Large Set of Audio Features for Sound Description; mathematical definitions are implemented independently. Source: link::https://recherche.ircam.fr/anasyn/peeters/ARTICLES/Peeters_2003_cuidadoaudiofeatures.pdf::.

These classes retain results between FFT frames and preserve the source FFT. Their cost is linear in bins, except SpecContrast's per-band sorting, which is O(M log M) for M bins in a band. They allocate work memory at construction and add no lookahead beyond the FFT window (21.3 ms for 1024 samples at 48 kHz).

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecFlatness::, this reads FFT timbre information; explicit band boundaries provide independent regional descriptors instead of one full-spectrum value.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain matching fftSize.
argument:: boundaries
Literal strictly increasing Array of 2..65 nonnegative frequencies in Hz. There is one band between each adjacent pair. The default uses octave bands from 200 Hz plus a bass and upper band; the final boundary is 20000 Hz, not a Nyquist sentinel.
argument:: normalize
Positive divides by the current band L2 norm; 0 gives raw flux.
argument:: positive
Positive keeps only increases; 0 includes rises and falls.
argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; 1 uses power.
argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192.
returns::
One value per band, with the optional SpecContrast valley vector appended.

examples::
FFTSubbandFlux2 measures spectral flux independently across specified frequency subbands. In this example, 4 subbands (`#[0, 250, 1500, 6000, 20000]`) monitor per-band transient activity. Sharp low-frequency flux (kick drum attack) triggers a sub-bass thump; high-frequency flux (hi-hat attack) triggers an airy stereo delay tap. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.bars:: displays real-time 4-subband flux.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 4);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var flux = FFTSubbandFlux2.kr(chain, boundaries: #[0, 250, 1500, 6000, 20000], normalize: 0, positive: 1, fftSize: 1024);
        var highTrig = (flux[3] > 0.4).lag(0.01, 0.08);
        var echo = DelayC.ar(source, 0.1, 0.08) * highTrig * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + (echo ! 2);

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, flux.clip(0, 5)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.bars("Subband Flux", ~miraBus, 4, 0.0, 2.0);
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
